WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.380
The air around you is about 78 percent nitrogen.

2
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.220
Yet corn farmers in Virginia usually add nitrogen to their fields.

3
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:19.100
Soybeans next door, with healthy root bacteria, can often get by with little or none.

4
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.600
So here is the question.

5
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.320
Why do farmers still have to give plants nitrogen?

6
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:28.423
Why does a plant need nitrogen at all?

7
00:00:29.343 --> 00:00:31.283
A protein is a large molecule.

8
00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:34.443
It builds cells and does much of their work.

9
00:00:35.483 --> 00:00:37.083
Proteins contain nitrogen.

10
00:00:38.803 --> 00:00:40.903
DNA carries a living thing's instructions.

11
00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:43.003
It contains nitrogen too.

12
00:00:43.863 --> 00:00:46.783
So a plant cannot grow new leaves without it.

13
00:00:47.433 --> 00:00:48.773
Here is the puzzle.

14
00:00:49.613 --> 00:00:51.633
The air's nitrogen is a gas.

15
00:00:52.513 --> 00:00:55.793
Most living things cannot use nitrogen in that form.

16
00:00:57.013 --> 00:01:01.313
Corn can stand in nitrogen-rich air all summer and still run short.

17
00:01:02.413 --> 00:01:05.613
Nitrogen gas must first be changed into other forms.

18
00:01:06.573 --> 00:01:07.953
Plants can use those forms.

19
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:14.033
Bacteria, lightning and factories can change nitrogen gas.

20
00:01:15.073 --> 00:01:19.053
Bacteria are tiny living things, each made of one cell.

21
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:21.853
Some kinds live free in the soil.

22
00:01:22.773 --> 00:01:25.793
They change nitrogen gas into a usable form.

23
00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:28.153
Lightning changes it too.

24
00:01:29.073 --> 00:01:30.933
So do factories that make fertilizer.

25
00:01:32.193 --> 00:01:36.173
Fertilizer is material farmers add to soil to supply nutrients.

26
00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:40.857
A root nodule is a small bump on a plant's root.

27
00:01:42.257 --> 00:01:45.677
Beans, peas, soybeans and peanuts can have them.

28
00:01:46.657 --> 00:01:48.297
Bacteria live inside it.

29
00:01:48.937 --> 00:01:52.777
They change nitrogen gas into a usable form for the plant.

30
00:01:53.617 --> 00:01:55.517
The plant gives the bacteria sugar.

31
00:01:56.477 --> 00:01:58.997
The bacteria give the plant usable nitrogen.

32
00:02:00.297 --> 00:02:01.897
Corn has no root nodules.

33
00:02:02.437 --> 00:02:05.317
It depends on usable nitrogen already in the soil.

34
00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.110
Plant roots take up usable nitrogen.

35
00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:13.370
Animals get nitrogen by eating plants or other animals.

36
00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.170
Decomposers break down dead things and wastes.

37
00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.730
They return the nitrogen to the soil.

38
00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:24.210
Other bacteria change some of it back into nitrogen gas.

39
00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:30.130
This movement, from air through living things and back, is the nitrogen cycle.

40
00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.450
So corn cannot use nitrogen gas.

41
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.330
It depends on usable nitrogen in soil.
